98132,198.932343,45.607742,200.132172,288.367828,200.132172,45.607742,185.932343,288.367828,185.932343,45.577728,187.132172,288.377716,187.132172,45.577728,172.932343,288.377716,172.932343,45.587723,174.132172,288.388000,174.132172,45.587723,159.932343,288.388000,159.932343,45.597748,161.132172,288.388031,161.132172,45.597748,146.932343,288.388031,146.932343,45.597733,148.132172,288.407990,148.132172,45.597733,133.932343,288.407990,133.932343,45.617752,135.132172,288.417908,135.132172,45.617752,120.932343,288.417908,120.932343,45.627747,122.132172,274.129150,122.132172,45.627747,107.932343,274.129150,107.932343" rect="45.133972,107.488586,291.221649,473.575928" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Coaches and Sport Scientists can use the methods presented&#xD;to predict 2000-m&#xD;on-water&#xD;rowing performance&#xD;in singles and women's coxless pairs with reasonable&#xD;confidence from measures of power output, stroke rate,&#xD;technical efficiency, headwind, race conditions, and&#xD;stroke-velocity&#xD;variability. The coefficients in Table 3&#xD;can be used by practitioners to estimate the mean power,&#xD;stroke rate, and technical efficiency an athlete or crew will&#xD;need to perform in order to achieve any given target race&#xD;outcome (assuming environmental conditions effect all&#xD;crews in a given race equally), providing a more detailed&#xD;understanding of the performance gap than differences&#xD;between current and target race performances in race&#xD;time or boat velocity alone. Knowledge of the mean power&#xD;and stroke rate needed to achieve a race performance goal&#xD;can be used to inform targeted training strategies, such&#xD;as the prescription of power and stroke rate in key on-water&#xD;high-intensity&#xD;sessions. Of the variables assessed,&#xD;power output had the greatest modifying effect on on-water&#xD;rowing performance, and therefore is an area where&#xD;substantial improvements in rowing race performance&#xD;can be achieved, as is stroke rate (albeit to a somewhat&#xD;lesser extent than power). Technical efficiency may have&#xD;a meaningful impact on performance but requires further&#xD;investigation. Nevertheless, comparison of the differing&#xD;contributions of power, stroke rate, and technical efficiency&#xD;to performance between crews can be used to identify&#xD;crew-specific&#xD;areas for performance improvement.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
